$topic{'start_menu'} = {
		title => 'The start menu', category => 'features',
		summary => 'Recently used apps, a category rail and a search.',
		body => [
			'The start menu opens on the apps you used most recently, with a category rail (accessories, business, games, media, system) for the whole catalogue.',
			'Its pages also cover what is running, what is upcoming and what is expiring, plus a System view of the house\'s levels: this machine\'s battery, every peer that answered the last pass, and the embedded boards any of them can see (over Wi-Fi, or the Bluetooth link a machine is already holding), each with charge, state, temperature, voltage and PPH, and an estimate of the time left on it (or the time to full while it is charging). The estimate is the pack\'s own gauge reading where it reports one, cross-checked against the measured drop: when the two disagree by more than half again, the percentage slope wins, since an Android charge counter is not in consistent units from one vendor to the next. Without a gauge it is a robust slope over up to three hours of discharging samples, shown as a range while the samples are noisy and as "more than" a floor when the pack is moving too slowly to measure. Every machine with a processor also reports its CPU load and the share of RAM in use, in green, amber or red as the load rises; on Android, where the kernel hides the whole-machine counters, the CPU figure is JawnOS\'s own share and says so. The top of the card also carries the current sync: how many rows it has moved out of how many, and the time left at the pace it is going. The view stays live while it is open, refreshing every ten seconds; a pack that reports its own gauge (a phone\'s charge counter, a laptop\'s energy and power) gives the finish time the moment it is plugged in, without waiting for a measured rate. A press on a peer\'s card opens the Assistant\'s remote_control panel already pointed at that machine, and a press on this machine\'s own card opens its Me view; the System page is also the quickest way into another machine\'s Manager or its remote keyboard and mouse. A search box can ride the menu, the taskbar or the dock. A new message raises a dot on the menu\'s mailbox icon.',
			'The Leave button at the foot of the menu opens a dialog of big choices. Exit is what leaving has always meant: the database is sealed into its backup and the session closes. On a computer the dialog also offers Restart, Shut down and Sleep, which ask the operating system to do exactly that; Sleep is the same suspend as closing a laptop\'s lid. The Android app offers Exit alone, because an ordinary app cannot restart or shut down the phone it runs on. If the database cannot be sealed, Exit refuses and keeps the house open rather than destroying the only copy.'
